{"type":"cards","design":"S_021","title":"Scope: what is in and what is out","lead":"Clear deployment boundaries focus the Vertex AI assistant on gate rebooking workflows without altering core flight systems.","items":[{"title":"In scope: departure gate agent workstation","text":"Embeds Vertex AI in departure control so gate agents view connection risks and rebook passengers without switching tools.","icon":"monitor-ai"},{"title":"In scope: reservation and seat APIs","text":"Connects via Apigee to passenger records, seat inventory, and fare rules for policy-compliant rebooking in disruptions.","icon":"plug-cable"},{"title":"In scope: domestic hub airport rollout","text":"Deploys first at a primary hub for live validation before expanding to all domestic gates using standard GKE containers.","icon":"airplane"},{"title":"Out of scope: passenger mobile channels","text":"Excludes direct passenger chat in mobile apps or kiosks, focusing exclusively on frontline gate agent workflows.","icon":"smartphone"},{"title":"Out of scope: crew and aircraft dispatch","text":"Leaves crew scheduling and maintenance in core control systems, consuming operational status as read-only feeds.","icon":"compass"},{"title":"Out of scope: core reservation replacement","text":"Preserves existing passenger service records, using the assistant as an orchestration layer requiring agent approval.","icon":"database"}],"notes":"Defining explicit boundaries keeps the delivery focused on the departure gate bottlenecks where missed connections and manual rebooking delays occur. By integrating with existing reservation APIs rather than replacing underlying flight systems, the project avoids core legacy dependencies while giving gate agents immediate decision support.

